The Human-English claims that a family of finitary operations on a set such as \(\omega\) determines an algebraic closure by forming the least subset that contains given elements and is closed under the operations, as with the subgroup generated by a set, the span of a set of vectors, or the convex hull of a set of points, and that these closures differ from topological closures by being continuous: an element of the closure of a set already lies in the closure of finitely many of those elements, which is the characteristic condition to be stated for \(\mathbf{P}\omega\). The imported definition `Closure` takes \(a\in\mathbf{P}\omega\) to be a closure exactly when the identity combinator lies in \(a\) and \(a\) is a retract (\(a=a\circ a\)), and `closure_expansive` turns that inclusion into \(x\subseteq a(x)\); subgroups, spans, and convex hulls are not defined, so the general finitary-operation closure is not the object formalized. The least-subset construction appears only for closing under one continuous operation: `Vapply` and `Vstep`, identified by `eq_5_14`, send \((a,x)\) to \(\bigcap\{y\mid x\subseteq y\text{ and }a(y)\subseteq y\}\) and equivalently to the least fixed point of \(y\mapsto x\cup a(y)\), with the fixed-point theorem supplying one inclusion because the intersection is a prefixpoint and the other because the fixed-point equation puts the iterate into that family; `Vapply_prefixpoint`, `Vapply_closed`, and `Vapply_eq_Vstep` then check that the value contains \(x\), contains \(a\) of itself, and equals the step applied to itself, while `eq_5_16` is \(x\subseteq\mathbf{V}(a)(x)\) and `eq_5_17` is idempotence of \(\mathbf{V}(a)\). Finite character of the fixed-point lattice is `closureRangeCountablyAlgebraic`: the fixed points of a closure form a countably algebraic lattice on the basis \(\{a(e_n)\}\), using `Isolated` (the compact elements of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\) are the finite sets \(e_n\)), `compact_fixed_iff` and `compact_fixed_iff_subset` (a fixed point is compact exactly when it equals \(a(e_n)\)), and `fixed_eq_sSup_compacts` with `compactFixedBelow_sUnion` (each fixed point is the directed supremum of the compact fixed points below it, identified with the retract basis by `retractBasis_eq_compactFixedBelow`). The compactness proof in `closureCompact_isCompactSubset` is the finitary step the subgroup example illustrates: expansiveness puts \(e_n\) under a directed cover, `directed_finite_cover_subset` finds one member containing that finite set, and monotonicity of application returns \(a(e_n)\) to that member. The representing closure is `representClosure`, \(x\mapsto\{m\mid d_m\sqsubseteq\bigsqcup\{d_n\mid n\in x\}\}\), and `representClosure_isScottContinuous` is continuity for that formula: finite support of the compact enumeration pulls an index in the closure back to a finite set of indices, and `representClosure_mono` lifts the converse; `representClosure_expansive`, `representClosure_idem`, and `representClosureCode_isClosure` make the code an expansive idempotent retract, and `representationOrderIso` is the order isomorphism, using `representationDecode_encode`. The remaining imported equations are the later calculus this note only cites. `eq_5_1` and `eq_5_2` are \(u\subseteq\lambda x.\,u(x)\) and \(\mathbf{I}\subseteq\mathbf{I}\circ\to\mathbf{I}\). `eq_5_8` is \(u=[[u]_0,[u]_1]\) for the disjoint pairing `squarePair`, and both projections are proved, but the sidecar of `eq_5_9` labels the first-projection theorem \([[x,y]]_0=x\) as Scott’s (5.9) and (5.10) together, while the second projection \([[x,y]]_1=y\) is the separate theorem `eq_5_10`. `eq_5_11` is \(u(x)\subseteq b(u(a(x)))\) by expansiveness of the two closures and monotonicity of application, and `Icomb_subset_arrowR` uses that inclusion, with (5.1), to put \(\mathbf{I}\) into \(a\circ\to b\). `boxTensor` with `boxTensor_isRetract` and `Icomb_subset_boxTensor`, and `boxPlus` with `boxPlus_expansive`, `boxPlus_isRetract`, and the occupancy evaluations `boxPlus_app_empty`, `boxPlus_app_left`, `boxPlus_app_right`, and `boxPlus_app_mixed`, are the product (5.12) and the sum (5.13): the sum proof splits into empty, left-only, right-only, and mixed components, rebuilding a one-sided element by expansiveness of `boxShift` and (5.8), and sending a mixed element to \(\top\). `boool_isClosure` is the modified boolean closure (5.3), whose idempotence `boool_idem` splits on whether \(0\) and a successor lie in the argument, and `funRetract_isClosure`, `intR_isClosure`, and `openR_isClosure` record that \(\mathbf{fun}\), \(\mathbf{int}\), and \(\mathbf{open}\) are closures. For the universe, `Vapply_graph_isClosure` shows that the graph of \(x\mapsto\mathbf{V}(a)(x)\) is a closure for every \(a\), `eq_5_18` and `eq_5_18_rev` give the two inclusions between \(a(x)\) and \(\mathbf{V}(a)(x)\) once \(a\) is a closure (`eq_5_18_eq` is the resulting equality), and `eq_5_21` with `Icomb_subset_Vcomb` give \(a\subseteq\mathbf{V}(a)\) and \(\mathbf{I}\subseteq\mathbf{V}\); the retract equation for the combinator \(\mathbf{V}\) itself is not among the imported stems. For the self-function-space example, `dEq` is the least fixed point of `selfArrowStep`, \(a\mapsto\mathbf{I}\cup(a\circ\to a)\), and `eq_5_25` proves only \(\mathbf{dEq}=\mathbf{I}\cup(\mathbf{dEq}\circ\to\mathbf{dEq})\) from that fixed-point equation; `eq_5_25_arrow` obtains \(\mathbf{dEq}=\mathbf{dEq}\circ\to\mathbf{dEq}\) solely under the extra hypothesis that \(\mathbf{I}\) is already contained in \(\mathbf{dEq}\circ\to\mathbf{dEq}\), by absorbing the union on one side and discarding the identity summand on the other. **Theorem:** partial — finite character of the representing closure and the decomposition of each fixed point as the supremum of the \(a(e_n)\) below it are proved, while the general finitary-operation closure is not defined, and \(d=d\circ\to d\) is proved only after assuming \(\mathbf{I}\subseteq d\circ\to d\). **Proof:** partial — `representClosure_isScottContinuous` and `directed_finite_cover_subset` are the finitary step the subgroup example illustrates, whereas the least-fixed-point comparison in `eq_5_14`, the occupancy split for the boxed sum, and the conditional absorption in `eq_5_25_arrow` are arguments the prose does not give.
